From 765ba6f38000fec607e3f56b9b2154539ef81c46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 21:27:01 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 25/40] lib/httpserver: compress responses with zstd for
 clients that prefer it

Responses are gzipped by gzhttp, which picks gzip whenever a client lists
both, so a reader of large exports cannot get zstd however it asks. Clients
whose Accept-Encoding weighs zstd above gzip, or the same and lists it
first, now get their responses compressed with zstd; every other client,
browsers and proxies included, keeps getting gzip exactly as before.

The zstd writer follows the rules gzhttp applies to gzip. It holds back the
first KiB and compresses only a successful full response at least that big
with no Content-Encoding or Content-Range of its own, so error messages and
small bodies go out as they are; HEAD, 204 and 206 are left alone,
informational statuses go out at once, a flush before anything is written
sends nothing, and a compressed response loses Accept-Ranges and gets Vary.
The request reaches the handler as it came, and nothing is written to a
connection after the response is aborted. The window is 1 MiB: an 80 MB
export compresses 15.2 times with it against 16.1 with the default 4 MiB,
while a response in flight holds a quarter of the memory.

The ACG ruler reads a day of raw samples through /api/v1/export and asks for
"zstd, gzip;q=0.5": zstd costs vmselect less CPU than gzip for the same
bytes, and the ruler decodes it faster.
